          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Blakers-Massey theorem
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
            is concerned with algebraically describing the first obstruction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excision"} excision
            for relative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            = succeed "." do
              %em Blakers-Massey connectivity theorem
          %h2#statement Statement
          %h3#traditional Traditional
          %p
            This
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
            is measured by triad homotopy groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ;
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_m(X;A,B)
            for a pointed space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with two subspaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
            each containing the base point. Here the group structure is defined for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \geq 3
            and is abelian for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \geq 4
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ;
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mo →
                    %mi ϵ
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ;
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to \pi_{n+1}(X;A,B) \to \pi_n (A, A \cap B) \to^{\epsilon} \pi_n( X,B) \to \pi_n(X;A,B) \to \cdots
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            is the excision map. The main result of Blakers and Massey is as follows:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Suppose the triad
              %strong X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ;
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =(X;A,B)
              is such that: (i) the interiors of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              cover
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C=A \cap B
              are connected; (iii) that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is simply connected; (iv) and that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,C)
              is
              = succeed "-connected" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (m-1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (B,C)
              is
              = succeed "-connected," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,n \geq 3
              %strong X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ;
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =(X;A,B)
              is
              = succeed "-connected" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (m+n-2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is simply connected then the morphism given by the generalised Whitehead product
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi m
                        %mo +
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ;
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{m}(A,C) \otimes \pi_{n}(B,C) \to \pi_{m+n-1}(X;A,B)
            %p
              %em> is an isomorphism
              \.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#BlakersMassey51"}> Blakers-Massey 51
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#tomDieck08"} tomDieck 08, theorem 6.4.1
          #InTermsOfPushouts.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              A more intrinsic statement in the language of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              of the connectivity part of the theorem is that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_2
              two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
              out of the same
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
              which are
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_1
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-connected+morphism"} connective
              and
              = succeed "-connective," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
              of their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi f
                                  %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi f
                                    %mn 2
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 2
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 2
                            %munder
                              %mo ×
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi Y
                                  %mn 2
                                %munder
                                  %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                                  %mi X
                                %msub
                                  %mi Y
                                  %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mi Y
                              %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;\stackrel{f_1}{\longrightarrow}&amp; Y_1
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{f_2}} &amp; \searrow
                    \\\
                    Y_2 &amp;&amp; Y_2 \underset{Y_2 \underset{X}{\coprod} Y_1}{\times} Y_1
                    }
            %p
              is
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n_1 + n_2 - 1)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-connected+morphism"} connective
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              For the special case that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi Y
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y_1 \simeq Y_2 \simeq \ast
              are point contractible, the Blakers-Massey theorem reduces to the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freudenthal+suspension+theorem"} Freudenthal suspension theorem
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Since the tensor product is zero if one of its factors is zero, this result also gives criteria for the excision morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
              to be an isomorphism in a certain range of dimensions. For this reason the excision consequences of that sequence are also called the
              %em excision theorem of Blakers and Massey
              and have been given quite separate proofs for example in (
              %a{:href => "#Hatcher"}> Hatcher
              ), and in (
              %a{:href => "#tomDieck08"}> tom Dieck
              ). The first non zero triad homotopy group is also called the
              = succeed "." do
                %em critical group
              %em algebraic topology
              one wants
              %em algebraic
              results, not just connectivity results.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              A natural question is what happens if the conditions that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m,n \geq 3
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              simply connected are weakened. For example in the case
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo =
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m=n=2
              we have the additional structure that the morphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_2(A,C) \to \pi_1(C), \pi_2(B,C) \to \pi_1(C)
              are crossed modules, and so the required relative homotopy groups are in general nonabelian. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \geq 3 ,n \geq 3
              then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_m(A,C), \pi_n(B,C)
              are still
              = succeed "-modules." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(C)
            %p
              The extension to the non simply connected case was given by Brown and Loday; one simply replaces the usual tensor product by the nonabelian tensor product of groups which act on each other and on themselves by conjugation. This result is a special case of a Seifert-van Kampen Theorem for
              = succeed "-cubes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %em structured space
              which allows for the development of algebraic structures which have structures in a range of dimensions.
            %p
              Thus one of the intuitions is that the Blakers-Massey Theorem, and hence also the FST, is of the Seifert-van Kampen type, since we are assuming that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the union of the interiors of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
          %h3#in_higher_topos_theory In higher topos theory
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The Blakers-Massey connectivity theorem in the form of remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InTermsOfPushouts"}
              holds in every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} (∞,1)-topos
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sheaves"} (∞,1)-sheaves
          %p
            This is shown in (
            %a{:href => "#Rezk10"}> Rezk 10, prop. 8.16
            ) with reference to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-sites"} (∞,1)-sites
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is announced in (
            %a{:href => "#HoTTBook"}> HoTTBook, theorem 8.10.2
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LumsdaineFinsterLicata13"} Lumsdaine-Finster-Licata 13
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
            code in (
            %a{:href => "#Favonia"}> Favonia 14
            ).
          %p
            This translates to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+language"} internal language
            proof of Blakers-Massey valid in all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            (including
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} elementary (∞,1)-toposes
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freudenthal+suspension+theorem"} Freudenthal suspension theorem
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Rezk14"} Rezk 14
          %h3#HigherCubical
            Higher cubical BM-theorems and analytic
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            There are higher analogs of the BM-theorem with (pushout) squares replaced by higher dimensional cubes. The higher BM-theorem (
            %a{:href => "#Goodwillie91"}> Goodwillie 91
            ) says equivalently that the identity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
            is a 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"}> analytic (∞,1)-functor
            \. See (
            %a{:href => "#MunsonVolic15"}> Munson-Volic 15, section 6
            ).
          %h2#references References
          %h3#classical Classical
          %p The original connectivity statement:
          %ul
            %li#BlakersMassey51
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Blakers"}> Albert Blakers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Massey"} William Massey
              %em The homotopy groups of a triad I
              , Annals of Mathematics 53: 161–204, (1951) (
              %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1969346"}> jstor:1969346
              )
          %p The algebraic statement and proof:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Albert+Blakers"}> Albert Blakers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Massey"} William Massey
              = succeed "," do
                %em The homotopy groups of a triad. {III}
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1969744"} jstor:1969744
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#tDKP70
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tammo+tom+Dieck"}> Tammo tom Dieck
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Heiner+Kamps"} Klaus Heiner Kamps
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dieter+Puppe"} Dieter Puppe
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopietheorie
                %strong 157
                Springer 1970 (
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/BFb0059721"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0059721
                )
            %li#Kochmann96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stanley+Kochmann"}> Stanley Kochmann
                , theorem 3.2.4 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bordism%2C+Stable+Homotopy+and+Adams+Spectral+Sequences"} Bordism, Stable Homotopy and Adams Spectral Sequences
            %li#Hatcher
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alan+Hatcher"}> Alan Hatcher
                , theorem 4.23
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html"} Algebraic Topology
            %li#tomDieck08
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tammo+tom+Dieck"}> Tammo tom Dieck
                , theorem 6.4.1
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic Topology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/diecktop.pdf"} pdf
          %p The Blakers-Massey’s Connectivity Theorem was shown to be a consequence of Farjoun’s “cellular inequalities”
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emmanuel+Dror+Farjoun"}> Emmanuel Dror Farjoun
              ,
              = succeed "m" do
                %em Cellular spaces, null spaces and homotopy localization
          %p is Theorem 1.B of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wojciech+Chach%C3%B3lski"}> Wojciech Chachólski
              ,
              %em A generalization of the triad theorem of Blakers-Massey
              Topology 36.6 (1997): 1381-1400
          %p
            This would constitute a purely
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy-theoretic
            proof.
          %p The generalisation of the algebraic statement is Theorem 4.3 in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronald+Brown"}> Ronald Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Loday"} Jean-Louis Loday
              = succeed "-cubes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %em Proc. London Math. Soc.
              (3) 54 (1987) 176-192 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://groupoids.org.uk/pdffiles/VKTEVANS2.pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p which relies essentially on the paper
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronald+Brown"} Ronald Brown
              and J.-L. Loday, Van Kampen theorems for diagrams of spaces,
              %em Topology
              26 (1987) 311-334,
          %p
            for the van Kampen Theorem and for the nonabelian tensor product of groups. Here is a link to a bibliography of 170 items on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://groupoids.org.uk/nonabtens.html"} nonabelian tensor product
          %p Further applications are explained in
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> R. Brown
            , Triadic Van Kampen theorems and Hurewicz theorems, _
          %p
            Algebraic Topology, Proc. Int. Conf. March 1988_, Edited M.Mahowald and S.Priddy, Cont. Math. 96 (1989) 39-57.
            %a{:href => "http://groupoids.org.uk//pdffiles/VKTEVANS2.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The following paper applies the methods of the above two Brown-Loday papers to the well known problem of
            = succeed "-ad" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %ul
            %li
              Ellis, G.J. and Steiner, R. Higher-dimensional crossed modules and the homotopy groups of
              = succeed "-ads." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
              %em J. Pure Appl. Algebra
              46 (1987) 117–136.
          %p
            The methods work because of their equivalence between cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^n
            \-groups and crossed
            = succeed "-cubes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-types:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %em broad
            categories for conjecturing and proving theorems, and
            %em narrow
            algebraic categories for calculations and relations with classical ideas. In this case the broad category is that of cat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ^n
            \-groups, and the narrow category is that of crossed
            = succeed "-cubes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-ads" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            = succeed "-types," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Loday"}> Jean-Louis Loday
              , Spaces with finitely many non-trivial homotopy groups,
              %em J. Pure Appl. Algebra
              24 (1982) 179-202.
          %p Further background to these ideas is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> R. Brown
              “A philosophy of modelling and computing homotopy types” Presentation to CT2015, Aveiro, Portugal, June 14-19.
              %a{:href => "https://groupoids.org.uk//pdffiles/aveiro-beamer-handout.pdf"} pdf
              and in “Modelling and computing homotopy types: I” Indag.Math. 29 (2018) 459-482
              %a{:href => "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0019357717300460"} pdf
          %p
            Discussion of Blakers-Massey connectivity for
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring+spectra"} ring spectra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+rings"} E-∞ rings
            and other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+over+operads"} algebras over operads
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Michael Ching,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Harper"} John Harper
              %em Higher homotopy excision and Blakers-Massey theorems for structured ring spectra
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1402.4775"}> arXiv:1402.4775
              )
          %p The higher cubical version of Blakers-Massey connectivity is due to
          %ul
            %li#Goodwillie91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Goodwillie"}> Tom Goodwillie
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Calculus II: Analytic functors
          %p a textbook account is in
          %ul
            %li#MunsonVolic15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Munson"}> Brian Munson
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ismar+Volic"} Ismar Volic
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cubical homotopy theory
              %a{:href => "http://palmer.wellesley.edu/~ivolic/pdf/Papers/CubicalHomotopyTheory.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#ReferencesInHoTT
            In
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            A proof of Blakers-Massey connectivity in general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-stack"} ∞-stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-toposes"} (∞,1)-toposes
            is in prop. 8.16 of
          %ul
            %li#Rezk10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"}> Charles Rezk
              ,
              %em Toposes and homotopy toposes
              (2010) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.uiuc.edu/~rezk/homotopy-topos-sketch.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            A general version of the connectivity theorem in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (and thus in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"} (infinity,1)-topos theory
          %ul
            %li#LumsdaineFinsterLicata13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"}> Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Finster"} Eric Finster
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"} Dan Licata
              (to appear)
          %p
            A fully computer-checked version of this proof in HoTT-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Agda"} Agda
            was produced in
          %ul
            %li#Favonia
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Favonia"}> Favonia
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://github.com/HoTT/HoTT-Agda/blob/1.0/Homotopy/BlakersMassey.agda"} BlakersMassey.agda
          %p the statement appeared also as
          %ul
            %li#HoTTBook
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Univalent+Foundations+Project"}> Univalent Foundations Project
              , theorem 8.10.2 of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Type+Theory+--+Univalent+Foundations+of+Mathematics"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
          %p and an announcement was given in
          %ul
            %li#Lumsdaine13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"}> Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
              ,
              %em The Blakers-Massey theorem in homotopy type theory
              talk at
              %a{:href => "http://www.crm.cat/en/Activities/Pages/ActivityFoldersAndPages/Curs%202013-2014/CHomotopy/chomotopy.aspx"} Conference on Type Theory, Homotopy Theory and Univalent Foundations
              (2013) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.crm.cat/en/Activities/Documents/AbstractsTypeTheory.pdf"}> talk abstracts pdf
              ).
          %p A writeup finally appeared as:
          %ul
            %li#FFLL16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kuen-Bang+Hou"} Kuen-Bang Hou
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Favonia"}> Favonia
              ),
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Finster"} Eric Finster
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"} Dan Licata
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+LeFanu+Lumsdaine"} Peter LeFanu Lumsdaine
              = succeed "," do
                %em A mechanization of the Blakers-Massey connectivity theorem in Homotopy Type Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.03227"} arXiv.1605.03227
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/2933575.2934545"} doi:10.1145/2933575.2934545
          %p
            Another unwinding to ordinary mathematical language of the above
            %a{:href => "#Favonia"} code
            was meanwhile given in
          %ul
            %li#Rezk14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"}> Charles Rezk
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Proof of the Blakers-Massey theorem
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://rezk.web.illinois.edu/freudenthal-and-blakers-massey.pdf"} pdf
          %p prompted by online discussion at
          %ul
            %li#Schreiber14
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Explaining the point of HoTT on FOM
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/DavidMichaelRoberts/Sandbox/blob/master/Schreiber_Gplus_post.md"} archived version
              %p
                (scroll down a fair bit through the list of replies there to see the exchange between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"} Charles Rezk
                and
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Favonia"} Favonia
          %p Further developments along these lines are in
          %ul
            %li#AnelBiedermanFinsterJoyal17a
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathieu+Anel"}> Mathieu Anel
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Biedermann"} Georg Biedermann
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Finster"} Eric Finster
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Generalized Blakers-Massey Theorem
                %strong 13
                4 (2020) 1521-1553
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.09050"} arXiv:1703.09050
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1112/topo.12163"} doi:10.1112/topo.12163
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (generalizing to the case where the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n-connected%2C+n-truncated%29+factorization+system"} (n-connected, n-truncated) factorization system
                  may be replaced by more general
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} modalities
            %li#AnelBiedermanFinsterJoyal17b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathieu+Anel"}> Mathieu Anel
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Biedermann"} Georg Biedermann
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Finster"} Eric Finster
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"} André Joyal
                %em Goodwillie’s Calculus of Functors and Higher Topos Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.09632"}> arXiv:1703.09632
                )
          %p
            Another proof and generalization in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            %span.newWikiWord
              Bass-Serre theory
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Bass-Serre+theory"} ?
            in such context, has been provided in
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Wärn24"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+W%C3%A4rn"}> David Wärn
              ,
              %em Path Spaces of Pushouts
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.12339"}> arXiv:2402.12339
              )
          %h3#in_shape_theory In shape theory
          %ul
            %li
              Šime Ungar,
              = succeed "-Connectedness" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %a{:href => "http://www.irb.hr/korisnici/zskoda/ungarConnwoabsh.pdf"} pdf
          %blockquote
            %p Let (X, A, x) be an n-connected inverse system of CW-pairs such that the restriction (A, x) is m-connected. We prove that there exists an isomorphic inverse system (Y, B, y) having n-connected terms such that the terms of the restriction (B, y) are m-connected. This result is then applied in proving analogues of Hurewicz and Blakers-Massey theorems for homotopy pro-groups and shape groups.
